WN.lb(45,{"mt_G1-8_1yC6w":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which part smells a flower?","choices":["Nose","Mouth","Ears"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sniff! Which part do you sniff with?","explain":"Your nose smells. Your mouth tastes and your ears hear."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which word tells how a drum sounds?","choices":["Loud","Fluffy","Sour"],"answer":0,"hint":"A drum is heard. Pick a word for ears.","explain":"Loud is a word for sounds. Fluffy is a feel word and sour is a taste word."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You find a mushroom in the park. What do you do?","choices":["Just look, do not taste","Taste a little bit","Put it in your pocket to eat later"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a park mushroom food from a grown-up?","explain":"Mushrooms outside can make you very ill. Look, but never taste."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A lemon tastes...","choices":["Sour","Loud","Bumpy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word is a taste word?","explain":"Sour is a taste word, and a lemon is sour. Loud is for ears and bumpy is for hands."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You hear {n} claps. Which part heard them?","choices":["Ears","Eyes","Mouth"],"answer":0,"hint":"Claps are sounds. Which part hears?","explain":"Ears hear sounds, so your ears heard the {n} claps.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which part tells you a pine cone is prickly?","choices":["Hands","Ears","Nose"],"answer":0,"hint":"Prickly is a feel word. Which part feels?","explain":"Your hands feel that the pine cone is prickly. Your nose smells it and your ears hear it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"There are {n} cakes. Which part tastes them?","choices":["Mouth","Ears","Eyes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part do you eat with?","explain":"Your mouth and tongue taste, so your mouth tastes the {n} cakes.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Is soap for tasting?","choices":["No, it is for washing","Yes, it smells nice","Yes, a little bit"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do you do with soap at the sink?","explain":"Soap is for washing. It may smell nice, but it is not food, so do not taste it."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which part of you hears a bell ring?","choices":["Ears","Eyes","Nose"],"answer":0,"hint":"A bell makes a sound. Which part takes in sounds?","explain":"Ears hear sounds, so your ears hear the bell. Eyes see it, but they can not hear it."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"You see {n} red balloons. Which part saw them?","choices":["Eyes","Ears","Hands"],"answer":0,"hint":"Seeing colours is a job for one part. Which one?","explain":"Eyes see colours, so your eyes saw the {n} red balloons.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A teddy is in a bag. You can not see it. How can you find out it is soft?","choices":["Feel it with your hand","You can not find out","Taste it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part can tell soft from hard?","explain":"Your hand can feel that it is soft, even with no looking. Never taste toys."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Gran gives you {n} grapes for snack. Is it OK to taste them?","choices":["Yes, food from a grown-up","No, never taste anything","Only if they are on the floor"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who gave you the grapes, and are grapes food?","explain":"Food a grown-up gives you is safe to taste, so you can eat the {n} grapes.","params":{"n":[3,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["You have five helpers on your body. Eyes see. Ears hear. Your nose smells. Your mouth tastes. Your hands feel.","Shut your eyes. Can you still find out? Yes! Your hands can feel a soft teddy. Your ears can hear a bell.","Say what you find. A drum is loud. A lamb is soft. A sock can be smelly. A pine cone is bumpy.","Look at a thing, feel it and sniff it. But do not taste it. Only taste food from a grown-up."],"adult":["Young children learn about the world through their senses. At two and three, the goal is to use looking, listening, touching, smelling and, when safe, tasting to explore everyday things, and to name the body part behind each sense: eyes, ears, nose, mouth (tongue) and hands (skin).","The lab gives each idea its own action. Children send a rainbow, bell, flower, strawberry and teddy to the body part that senses them, find toys in a feely bag by touch alone, choose sense words such as loud, sour, soft and smelly, sort food from things that are only for looking, and explore a pine cone with three senses while the mouth stays shut.","Common mistakes: mixing up which part does what (smelling with the mouth, hearing with the eyes); thinking you can only find out by looking; using a word from the wrong sense, such as calling a drum fluffy; and putting non-food things in the mouth. Touch is felt by skin all over the body; hands are just the part children use most.","Keep the safety rule simple and firm: we taste only food a grown-up gives us. Berries, mushrooms and plants outside, soap, pills and small objects are for looking, not tasting."]},"familyTip":"On a walk, collect one safe natural thing, such as a pine cone, leaf or stone. Ask, \"What can your eyes tell you? Your hands? Your nose?\" Give one new word each time: bumpy, smooth, crunchy, smelly.","keyIdea":"You have five senses. Eyes see, ears hear, your nose smells, your mouth tastes and your hands feel. Use them to find out about things. Only taste food that a grown-up gives you.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which sense does each word belong to? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["👂 Hear","✋ Feel","👃 Smell"],"items":[["loud",0],["quiet",0],["squeaky",0],["soft",1],["bumpy",1],["prickly",1],["smelly",2],["stinky",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsense","caption":"Flagship lab: match things to body parts, find toys by touch, pick sense words, sort food from look-only things and explore a pine cone.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_a6UA5XD1SU":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which changes faster in the sun?","choices":["An ice cube","A banana","A rock"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about which one you can watch change before lunch.","explain":"An ice cube melts fast, in about an hour in the sun. A banana takes days and a rock does not change."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Before, the flower was shut. Now it is open. Is it the same flower?","choices":["Yes, it changed","No, a new flower","It did not change"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did someone swap it, or did the same flower open up?","explain":"Yes, it is the same flower. It changed by opening up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ava looks at a leaf and a teddy at {n} o’clock and again at bedtime. The teddy is the same. Is that OK to say?","choices":["Yes, some things stay the same","No, everything changes","No, teddies melt"],"answer":0,"hint":"Noticing what stays the same is part of looking closely.","explain":"Yes. Good scientists say what changed and what stayed the same, like Ava did from {n} o’clock to bedtime.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Put these in order: white apple slice, a bit brown, very brown. Which is first?","choices":["The white slice","The very brown slice","The bit brown slice"],"answer":0,"hint":"The slice gets browner the longer it is left out.","explain":"The white slice is first. Then it goes a bit brown, and now it is very brown."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"How can you see small things on a bug?","choices":["Look up close with a magnifier","Look fast from far away","Close your eyes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which way makes tiny details big?","explain":"Look up close with a magnifier. A quick look from far away misses small details."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Sam has {n} ice cubes in a bowl. After a while they are gone and the bowl has water. Where did the ice go?","choices":["It melted into water","Into the sky","Sam threw it out"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is in the bowl now that was not there before?","explain":"It melted. All {n} ice cubes turned into the water in the bowl.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which words tell about a change?","choices":["Before and now","Red and blue","Up and down"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which words talk about time?","explain":"Before and now, or first, then and now, help you say what changed over time."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A puddle is big in the morning and gone at night. What happened?","choices":["It dried up","It grew","Nothing changed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare morning and night. What is different?","explain":"It dried up. The puddle got smaller and smaller until it was gone."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Ice sits in a warm cup. Later the cup has water and no ice. What happened?","choices":["The ice melted","The ice went away","The cup made water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where did the water in the cup come from?","explain":"The ice melted. It turned into the water in the cup, so it did not go away."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Mia checks a banana for {n} days. First yellow, then spots. What comes next?","choices":["Brown all over","Green again","Yellow again"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bananas get darker as they get older, not lighter.","explain":"Next it goes brown all over. After {n} days or so, a banana keeps getting browner; it does not go back.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which tool helps you see a tiny seed in an apple?","choices":["A magnifier","A cup","A spoon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which tool makes small things look big?","explain":"A magnifier, also called a hand lens, makes tiny things look big so you can see the detail."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Leo looks at a puddle at {n} o’clock and again after lunch. It is smaller. What can he say?","choices":["Before it was big, now it is small","It is the same","It got bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use before and now to say what is different.","explain":"Before it was big, now it is small. Saying before and now describes the change Leo saw since {n} o’clock.","params":{"n":[8,11]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Put ice in a cup. Wait. Look again! The ice is water now. Before it was hard. Now it is wet.","Look at a banana each day. First it is yellow. Then it gets spots. Now it is brown.","A magnifier makes small things look big. Look up close. You may see a tiny bug or a seed.","Some things change fast. Some change slow. Some do not change at all. Look and say what you see."],"adult":["At three and four, science is mostly careful looking. Children use their senses to notice details, compare two things, and say how something changed over time. Development Matters asks three and four year olds to talk about the differences between materials and changes they notice, and the early learning goal for the natural world asks them to explore and describe what they see.","Give your child the words of observation: before and now; first, then and now; the same and different; bigger and smaller. A hand lens, or magnifier, makes small details easy to see. Looking at the same thing a few times shows that some changes are fast, like ice melting in about an hour on a warm day, and some are slow, like a banana going brown over several days.","Common mistakes to watch for: thinking melted ice has gone away, when it has turned into the water in the cup; thinking a changed thing is a new thing; noticing only big changes and missing small ones; and mixing up which picture came first. Each time, ask: what was it like before, and what is it like now?","In the lab your child slides time on to melt ice, spots three changes between a Before and a Now picture, and notices what stayed the same. Then they move a magnifier over an apple slice to find tiny things, step through an hour, a day and a week, and put pictures in first, then, now order."]},"familyTip":"Put an apple slice and an ice cube on two plates. Look at them now, after lunch and at bedtime. Ask what is different now from before, and draw or photo each one to compare.","keyIdea":"Scientists look closely and notice what changes. Say what it was like before and what it is like now. Some changes are fast, like ice melting. Some are slow, like a banana going brown.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Did it change? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🔄 It changed","🟰 Still the same"],"items":[["Ice turned to water",0],["Banana went brown",0],["Puddle got smaller",0],["Flower opened",0],["Teddy looks the same",1],["Ball looks the same",1],["Cup looks the same",1],["Spoon looks the same",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagchange","caption":"Flagship lab: watch ice melt, spot what changed, look up close with a magnifier, see fast and slow changes, and order pictures first, then, now.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_MLnph38ePa":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Your guess was wrong. Is that OK?","choices":["Yes. A guess helps you start.","No. Never guess.","No. Guesses must be right."],"answer":0,"hint":"Do scientists always guess right the first time?","explain":"A guess does not have to be right. You guess, then you find out the real answer."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which thing will stick to a magnet?","choices":["A steel paper clip","A teddy","A plastic cup"],"answer":0,"hint":"Magnets pull iron and steel.","explain":"The paper clip is steel, so it sticks. Cloth and plastic do not."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Shiny foil does not stick to a magnet. Why?","choices":["Foil is not iron or steel","It is too shiny","The magnet is asleep"],"answer":0,"hint":"Shiny is not the thing that matters. What is foil made of?","explain":"Magnets pull iron and steel. Foil is a different metal, so it does not stick."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A ladybird sits on a leaf in front of you. How many spots has it got? How can you find out?","choices":["Look closely and count","Ask the ladybird","Guess and stop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you see the spots? Then you do not need to ask.","explain":"You can see the spots, so look closely and count them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which word often starts a wonder question?","choices":["Why","Yes","Look"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word asks for a reason?","explain":"\"Why\" asks for a reason. How and what if start wonder questions too."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Where do penguins live? You have never seen one. How can you find out?","choices":["Ask a grown-up or look in a book","Look out of the window","Try it in the bath"],"answer":0,"hint":"Penguins are far away. Who or what could tell you?","explain":"You cannot see penguins from here, so ask someone or look in a book."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You had {n} wonder questions. You found out the answer to 1. How many are still to find out?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take the 1 you found out away from {n}.","explain":"{n} take away 1 is {=n-1}. Keep looking, trying and asking!","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Tom asks \"Why?\" lots of times. What do you think?","choices":["Good. Asking helps Tom learn.","Silly. Tom should stop.","Naughty."],"answer":0,"hint":"How do we find out new things?","explain":"Asking why is how you find out. It is a good thing to do."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"{n} things stuck to the magnet. You test 1 more steel pin. It sticks too. How many stuck now?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Steel sticks, so count on 1 from {n}.","explain":"{n} and 1 more is {=n+1}. The steel pin stuck, like the other steel things.","params":{"n":[1,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which one is a wonder question?","choices":["Why do birds sing?","Birds can sing.","I see a bird."],"answer":0,"hint":"A question asks something. Look for why, how or what if.","explain":"\"Why do birds sing?\" asks for a reason. The other two tell you a thing."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You test {n} things on a magnet. 2 of them stick. How many do not stick?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Start with the {n} things and take away the 2 that stick.","explain":"{n} take away 2 is {=n-2}. Testing each one showed you which stick.","params":{"n":[4,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Will my toy boat float? What is the best way to find out?","choices":["Put it in water and see","Look at it very hard","Stop wondering"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you try this one yourself?","explain":"You can try it! Putting the boat in water shows you if it floats."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You asked {n} why questions at the park and 1 more at home. How many why questions did you ask?","answer":"n+1","hint":"You asked {n}, then 1 more. Count on 1 from {n}.","explain":"{n} and 1 more is {=n+1}. Every question is a chance to find out.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Look at a snail. Do you wonder why it has a shell? That wonder is a question. Questions often start with why, how or what if.","Next, have a go at a guess. Maybe the shell keeps it safe. Your guess can be wrong. That is fine. A guess helps you start.","Then find out. You can look closely. You can try it and see. Or you can ask a grown-up, or look in a book.","Some things fool us. Shiny foil looks like it should stick to a magnet. But when you try it, it does not. Trying it showed you the real answer."],"adult":["This is the first step of scientific inquiry. A child notices something, asks a question, offers a guess and then looks for evidence. In England, Development Matters (2023) sets out what 3 and 4 year olds do. They use all their senses to explore natural materials. They explore how things work. They explore and talk about forces they can feel, such as a magnet's pull. In Communication and Language, they learn to understand why questions. The Characteristics of Effective Learning add having their own ideas, making predictions and testing them. In the US, the Head Start Early Learning Outcomes Framework goal P-SCI 4 says the child asks a question, gathers information, and makes predictions. At 36 to 48 months that means simple questions, adults as the main source of answers, and simple predictions with support.","Young children ask a great many questions. Chouinard (2007) recorded children aged 1 to 5 asking dozens of information-seeking questions an hour in everyday talk with parents. Frazier, Gelman and Wellman (2009) found that preschoolers notice when they get a non-answer: after a real explanation they tended to agree or ask a follow-up, but after a non-answer they often asked again or offered their own explanation. So \"why\" is usually a genuine request for a cause, not stalling.","Common mistakes: brushing off why questions with \"because it is\", which teaches that questions are a nuisance. Answering so fast that the child never makes a guess of their own. Treating a wrong guess as a failure, so the child stops guessing. A common slip in the science itself is believing that all shiny or metal things stick to a magnet. Only some metals, mainly iron and steel, are pulled; aluminium foil is not.","In the lab your child taps things in a garden to wonder about them. Then they sort asking sentences from telling ones. They make a guess before they hear each answer. They test seven things on a magnet, and they are stopped if they try to sort a thing before testing it. Last, they match questions to a way to find out: look, try it, or ask."]},"familyTip":"When your child asks why, try \"What do you think?\" before you answer, and welcome any guess. Then find out together: look closely, try it, or look it up. A fridge magnet and a few things from a drawer make a quick test of the shiny-foil surprise.","keyIdea":"Scientists start by wondering. You notice something, ask a why, how or what-if question, and have a go at a guess. Then you find out by looking closely, trying it, or asking someone. A guess does not have to be right. It is how finding out begins.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it asking, or telling? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["❓ Asking","💬 Telling"],"items":[["Why do cats purr?",0],["Cats have fur.",1],["How do plants drink?",0],["The grass is green.",1],["What if the sun went out?",0],["It is raining today.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcurio","caption":"Flagship lab: wonder about things in a garden, sort asking from telling, guess first, test things on a magnet, and choose how to find out.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_sjbKGJB9vz":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A ladybird has {a} spots on one wing and {b} spots on the other. How many spots should your drawing have?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Count the spots on both wings and add them.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}, so your drawing should have {=a+b} spots, the same as the real one.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Sam drew a flower in {n} minutes. What is a good thing to do next?","choices":["Look at the real flower again","Stop, it is finished","Add a face to the flower"],"answer":0,"hint":"Scientists check their drawing against the real thing.","explain":"Look again. You may spot something new, like the lines on the petals, and add it.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You want to draw a worm. Where should you look?","choices":["At a real worm","At a cartoon worm","Nowhere, I know what worms look like"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one shows what is really there?","explain":"Look at a real worm. A cartoon worm may have a face, but a real worm has no eyes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"How many legs does a ladybird have?","choices":["6","4","8"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the legs on the real ladybird in the lab.","explain":"A ladybird has 6 legs, like every insect. So your drawing gets 6 legs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which drawing shows what is really there?","choices":["A spider with 8 legs and no face","A spider with 6 legs and a smile","A spider with shoes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the legs. Do real spiders smile?","explain":"A real spider has 8 legs and no smiley face, so that drawing shows what is really there."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ben forgets what colour the bee's stripes are. What should he do?","choices":["Look back at the real bee","Guess a colour","Leave the stripes out"],"answer":0,"hint":"The real bee is right there. Can he check?","explain":"He should look back. The stripes are black, so he can copy them and not guess."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why do scientists draw what they see?","choices":["To show what is really there","To make it look funny","To fill the page"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what a looking drawing is for.","explain":"A scientist's drawing shows what is really there, so other people can see what they found."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Lily's snail drawing has a shell, feelers and a smiley mouth. Which part should she rub out?","choices":["The smiley mouth","The shell","The feelers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part is not on a real snail?","explain":"A real snail has a shell and feelers, but no smiley mouth, so she should rub out the mouth."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A real spider has 8 legs. Your spider drawing has {n} legs so far. How many more legs do you need to draw?","answer":"8-n","hint":"Count on from your number up to 8.","explain":"8 take away {n} is {=8-n}, so you need {=8-n} more legs to match the real spider.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Mia looks at a real snail for {n} seconds. Then she draws legs on her snail. What should she do?","choices":["Look again and rub out the legs","Add more legs","Add a smiley face too"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does a real snail have legs? Look back at it.","explain":"A real snail has no legs. It slides on one big foot. Mia should look again and rub the legs out.","params":{"n":[5,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The real leaf is green. What colour should your leaf drawing be?","choices":["Green","My favourite colour","Purple, to look pretty"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look back at the real leaf. What colour do you see?","explain":"Green, because a looking drawing uses the colours of the real thing."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"You want to see the tiny hairs on a bee. What can help?","choices":["A magnifying glass","Closing my eyes","Standing far away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one makes small things look bigger?","explain":"A magnifying glass makes small parts look bigger, so you can see them and draw them."}],"explain":{"kid":["Scientists draw to show what they see. They look hard at a real bug or plant. Then they draw it.","Look, then draw. Count the legs on a ladybird. It has 6. So your drawing gets 6 legs too.","Draw only what is there. A real snail has no legs and no smiley mouth. It has a shell and feelers. So leave out the made-up bits.","Then look again. You may spot more, like stripes on a bee. A magnifying glass makes small things look big."],"adult":["In England, one early years goal for The Natural World says children explore the world around them, make observations and draw pictures of animals and plants. In Key Stage 1, working scientifically includes observing closely and using simple equipment, such as a hand lens.","A science drawing is a record, not a decoration. The habit we want is a loop: look at the object, draw one part, look back to check it, and repeat. Counting parts, copying real colours and noticing shapes turn looking into evidence your child can talk about.","Common mistakes: drawing a symbol from memory, like a cartoon snail with a face, instead of the real one in front of them. Children also add legs, smiles or eyelashes that are not there, guess numbers and colours, and stop after one quick look. A gentle question helps: show me where you can see that on the real one.","In the lab your child counts the legs on a ladybird and gives their drawing the same number. They rub out made-up parts on a snail, colour a flower by looking back, and move a magnifier over a bee to find wings, stripes and feelers. Last, they pick which drawings show what is really there."]},"familyTip":"Pick up one leaf, shell or bug on a walk and draw it together, side by side. Before you start, count something: how many points, legs or lines? Halfway through, stop and say, let us look again. Then ask your child to show you one thing they saw on the real one that they had not drawn yet.","keyIdea":"A scientist draws what is really there. First look at the real thing, then draw, then look again. Count the legs, copy the real colours, and leave out made-up things like smiley faces. A magnifying glass helps you see small parts you missed.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which parts are on a real snail, and which are made up? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["On a real snail","Made up"],"items":[["a shell",0],["feelers",0],["one big foot",0],["a slime trail",0],["legs",1],["a smiley mouth",1],["eyelashes",1],["shoes",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaglook","caption":"Flagship lab: count legs and draw the same number, rub out made-up parts, colour by looking back and use a magnifier.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_y0ffQfjUli":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why does a wind-up toy walk?","choices":["Its spring was wound up","It wants to walk","It is magic"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did you do to the key before it walked?","explain":"Turning the key winds a spring inside. When you let go, the spring makes the toy walk."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How do you open a peg?","choices":["Squeeze its ends","Pull its mouth apart","Blow on it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Press the two long ends together.","explain":"Squeezing the ends opens the mouth. Let go and the spring shuts it tight."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which part of a zip do you pull?","choices":["The little tab","The coat","The bottom of the zip"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is the small part you can hold.","explain":"Pull the little tab. It moves the slider, and the slider joins the two sides."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The toy stops walking. How can it walk again?","choices":["Wind it up again","Wait and it will go by itself","Paint it a new colour"],"answer":0,"hint":"What made it walk the first time?","explain":"The spring has run down. Turn the key again to wind it up, and it will walk again."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You wind the toy {a} times, then {b} times. How many more turns was the second go?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Start at {a} and count up to {b}.","explain":"From {a} up to {b} is {=b-a} more turns, so the second go walks further.","params":{"a":[1,2],"b":[3,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"There are {n} socks. You have pegged {k} on the line. How many still need a peg?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Start at {n} and take away the {k} that are hanging.","explain":"{n} take away {k} leaves {=n-k} socks still to peg.","params":{"n":[4,6],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"You do not know how a box opens. What is a good thing to do?","choices":["Try one part and see what it does","Give up","Hit it hard"],"answer":0,"hint":"How did you find out what the tap did?","explain":"Try one part gently, watch what happens, then try the next part. That is how you find out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You press a light switch. What happens?","choices":["The light goes on or off","The door opens","Water comes out"],"answer":0,"hint":"A switch is like the torch button.","explain":"A light switch turns the light on, or off if it was on. Each switch does one job."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which part of a torch turns the light on?","choices":["The button","The handle","The glass at the front"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part moves when you press it?","explain":"The button is the switch. Press it and the light comes on. Press it again and it goes off."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You turn the toy's key {a} times. Then you turn it {b} more times. How many turns is that?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Start at {a} and count on {b} more turns.","explain":"{a} turns and {b} more turns make {=a+b} turns. More turns wind the spring tighter.","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[1,2]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"There are {n} socks to hang up. Each sock needs 1 peg. How many pegs do you need?","answer":"n","hint":"Count 1 peg for every sock.","explain":"One peg for each sock, so {n} socks need {n} pegs.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"You turn the tap more. What happens?","choices":["More water comes out","Less water comes out","The water stops"],"answer":0,"hint":"Turning back makes the water stop. What does turning more do?","explain":"Turning the tap handle more lets more water out. Turning it back makes less, then none."}],"explain":{"kid":["Things have parts. Each part has a job. A torch has a button. Press it and the light comes on. Press it again and the light goes off.","Some parts turn. Turn a tap a little, and a little water comes out. Turn it more, and more water comes out. Turn it back, and it stops.","A wind-up toy has a key. Turn the key and a spring inside gets tight. Let go and the toy walks. Turn the key more, and it walks further. It is not magic. The spring makes it go.","How does a thing work? Try one part. Watch what it does. Then try the next part. Ask a grown-up first if it is hot, sharp or plugged in."],"adult":["Development Matters, the guidance for England's Early Years Foundation Stage, lists 'Explore how things work' for 3- and 4-year-olds in Understanding the World. The skill is noticing cause and effect: I do this to this part, and that happens. It feeds later science, such as pushes and pulls, simple circuits and asking questions, and early design and technology, where children take things apart to see how they work.","Pick safe things with one clear moving part: a torch switch, a tap, a wind-up toy, a zip, a clothes peg, a jar lid, a door handle. Let your child try first, then talk about it: what did you do, which part moved, what happened. Words to use: press, turn, twist, pull, squeeze, wind, button, handle, spring, switch, on, off, more, less.","Common mistakes are explanations like 'the toy wants to walk' or 'it's magic'. Young children often give objects feelings or treat a result as magic. Gently replace these with the part that caused it: 'you wound the spring'. Another slip is thinking a toy stops because it is tired or sad, not because the spring has run down. Be careful to keep this hands-on but safe: no plug sockets, hot taps or sharp blades.","In the lab your child presses a torch button on and off, turns a tap a little and a lot to fill a cup, winds a toy once and then three times to see it go further, pulls a zip up by its tab, and squeezes a peg to hang three socks."]},"familyTip":"Go on a 'how does it work?' hunt at home. Find three things with a part that moves: a light switch, a bottle top, a peg. For each one, ask 'What do you think will happen?' before your child tries it.","keyIdea":"Everyday things have parts, and each part does a job. Press a torch button and the light comes on. Turn a tap handle more and more water comes out. Wind a toy's key and its spring makes it go. Pull a zip's tab and the two sides join. Squeeze a peg and its mouth opens. To find out how something works, try one part at a time and watch what happens: that is cause and effect.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Do you press it or turn it? Drag each thing into a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Press it","Turn it"],"items":[["Light switch",0],["Doorbell",0],["Torch button",0],["Lift button",0],["Tap handle",1],["Jar lid",1],["Key on a wind-up toy",1],["Bottle top",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagworks","caption":"Flagship lab: press a torch button, turn a tap a little and a lot, wind up a toy, pull a zip and squeeze a peg to find each part's job.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Hw70LI5xza":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Water drops on glass are…","choices":["an observation","proof the jar is sad","a feeling"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask what your eyes actually saw on the glass.","explain":"Drops are sensed; sadness is a story."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Best evidence a plant grew?","choices":["height 4 cm then 7 cm","the plant looks proud","I feel it grew"],"answer":0,"hint":"Prefer a number you measured with a ruler.","explain":"Measured height change is observation evidence."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which can you check by looking?","choices":["The leaf is green","The leaf is jealous","The leaf wants friends"],"answer":0,"hint":"Colour is something you can see and check.","explain":"Green is checkable; jealousy is not."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Observation and interpretation…","choices":["are different and both useful","are the same","should never be written"],"answer":0,"hint":"Scientists use both — labelled clearly.","explain":"Keep them distinct so claims rest on evidence."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Thermometer shows {t} °C. What temperature did you observe?","answer":"t","hint":"Read the number shown on the instrument.","explain":"{t} °C is the observation from the thermometer.","params":{"t":[10,30]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Count {a} red blocks and {b} blue. How many blocks observed in all?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the red and blue counts you can see.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} blocks observed.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why separate observation from interpretation?","choices":["so claims rest on what was sensed","so you never guess","because observations are wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Evidence underpins claims.","explain":"Guesses are fine when labelled; claims need observations."},{"type":"order","id":"m8","prompt":"Order: talk about a dog bark carefully.","items":["say what you heard (the bark)","say your guess (maybe it wants food)","keep the guess labelled as a guess"],"hint":"Start from the sense data you heard.","explain":"Report the bark, then the guess, clearly separated."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"\"The ice is melting\" is…","choices":["an observation","an interpretation","neither"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you see or measure it?","explain":"Melting is what you sense — an observation."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"\"The dog was angry\" after a bark is…","choices":["an interpretation","a pure observation","a measurement"],"answer":0,"hint":"You heard a bark; anger is a meaning you added.","explain":"Anger is an interpretation layered on the bark."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A plant was {a} cm, now {b} cm. Growth in cm?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Subtract start height from new height.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a} cm — an observation used as evidence.","params":{"a":[3,6],"b":[7,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You record {n} beeps. How many did you hear?","answer":"n","hint":"The count is the observation.","explain":"You heard {n} beeps — that count is the observation.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Order steps to check a claim carefully.","items":["write what you observed","write your guess separately","see if the observation supports the guess"],"hint":"Facts first, then meaning.","explain":"Separate observation from interpretation, then test the link."}],"explain":{"kid":["An observation is what you see, hear, or measure.","An interpretation is a guess about why or what it means.","\"The dog barked\" is what you heard. \"It was angry\" is a guess.","Good science claims need observations as evidence."],"adult":["Working scientifically (KS1/KS2): an observation is what you see, hear or measure. An interpretation is a guess about why. Words: observe, evidence, claim, measure.","Common mistakes: calling a feeling an observation; using \"I feel it grew\" as evidence; treating observation and interpretation as the same.","In the lab your child sorts clues, names water drops (not a homesick jar), rejects angry as an observation, and links growth to measured height."]},"familyTip":"Look out the window together. Ask: 'What do you see?' then 'What do you think is going on?' Label each sentence as see or think.","keyIdea":"An observation is what you see, hear, or measure. An interpretation is a guess about why or what it means. Keep them separate so claims rest on evidence.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Observation or interpretation? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Observation","Interpretation"],"items":[["The ice is melting",0],["The ice feels sad",1],["I hear a beep",0],["The machine is lonely",1],["The leaf is green",0],["The leaf is jealous",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagobsvs","caption":"Flagship lab: sort see/hear from guesses, name jar drops, reject angry-as-observation, match height evidence, then challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_XirhnAB6Ye":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is a science question?","choices":["Do snails like wet places?","Which colour is the best?","What is your name?"],"answer":0,"hint":"A science question is about how the world works, and we can find out.","explain":"We can find out if snails like wet places by watching them. Which colour is best is only what you like."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Do ice cubes melt faster in the sun or in the shade? Best way to find out?","choices":["Test it with two ice cubes","Just guess","Ask which is nicer"],"answer":0,"hint":"We can try this ourselves.","explain":"Put one ice cube in the sun and one in the shade, then look. That test shows which melts faster."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"How many moons does Jupiter have? Best way to find out?","choices":["Look it up in a book","Test it","Count them from the garden"],"answer":0,"hint":"Jupiter is very far away.","explain":"Jupiter is too far to test, and most of its moons are too small to see from a garden. We look it up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What does a caterpillar turn into? Best way to find out?","choices":["Watch it for many weeks","Look at it once","Test it with a ball"],"answer":0,"hint":"The change is slow, so one look will not show it.","explain":"A caterpillar changes slowly into a butterfly. We have to watch it again and again to see it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which question can science NOT answer?","choices":["Which song is the nicest?","Do plants need water?","Where does a puddle go?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the one that is about what you like.","explain":"Nicest is what you like, and people like different songs. We can find out the other two."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Ben asks {a} questions in the garden. Then he asks {b} more at the park. How many questions did he ask?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the garden questions and the park questions.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} questions. Asking lots of questions is how scientists start.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Ella guesses that the big ball bounces highest. What should she do next?","choices":["Drop the balls to find out","Say she is right","Pick a different guess"],"answer":0,"hint":"A guess is a start, not an answer.","explain":"A guess is not finding out. Ella should test it by dropping each ball from the same height."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Zoe looks at her frog tank once a week. She looks for {a} weeks, then {b} more weeks. How many times did she look?","answer":"a+b","hint":"One look each week. Add the weeks together.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} looks. Frogspawn changes slowly, so Zoe needs to look many times.","params":{"a":[3,6],"b":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which ball bounces highest? How can we find out?","choices":["Just guess","Drop each ball and look","Ask which ball is the nicest"],"answer":1,"hint":"We can try this ourselves, right now.","explain":"Drop each ball from the same height and watch how high it bounces. That is a test."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"How does a bean seed grow into a plant? How can we find out?","choices":["Watch it for many days","Look at it once","Pick the best bean"],"answer":0,"hint":"A plant grows slowly. One look is not enough.","explain":"A bean grows a little each day. We watch it for many days to see the root, the shoot and the leaves."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Mia looks at her bean plant on {a} days. Then she looks on {b} more days. On how many days did she look?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Count the first days, then count on the extra days.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} days. Watching on many days shows how the plant changes.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"How far away is the Sun? How can we find out?","choices":["Measure it with a ruler","Look it up in a book","Watch it for a day"],"answer":1,"hint":"The Sun is much too far away to measure or test.","explain":"We cannot reach the Sun. Scientists have found out how far it is, so we look it up in a book or ask an expert."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"Sam drops the tennis ball {a} times and the sponge ball {a} times. How many drops is that in all?","answer":"2*a","hint":"Add the tennis ball drops and the sponge ball drops.","explain":"{a} + {a} = {=2*a} drops. Doing the test more than once helps Sam be sure.","params":{"a":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Scientists ask questions. How does it work? Why does it happen? Where does it go? A science question is one we can find out the answer to.","Some answers we find by watching. Frogspawn turns into tadpoles, then frogs. We have to look again and again for weeks to see it.","Some answers we find with a test. Which ball bounces highest? Drop each ball from the same height and look. Some answers are too far away to watch or test, like how far the Moon is. Then we look in a book or ask an expert.","Some questions are not for science. Which colour is best? That is what you like, and people like different things."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 8. This matches KS1 Working scientifically in England: ask simple questions and see that they can be answered in different ways. It also fits NGSS K-2-ETS1-1, which asks children to ask questions and gather information. It builds on Wondering and asking why and on Observation vs Interpretation.","The three ways here are the KS1 types of enquiry in simple words. Watching is observing over time, as with frogspawn. Testing is a simple comparative or fair test, as with balls dropped from the same height. Looking it up is research using books, websites or an expert, as with the distance to the Moon.","Common mistakes: thinking a guess is the same as finding out; looking only once at something that changes slowly; trying to test things that are too big or far away; and treating 'which is best?' as a science question. Be careful to praise good questions, not only right answers.","In the lab your child taps garden things to ask questions, looks at a frog tank four times over about 12 weeks, drops three balls from the same height, looks up the Moon, sorts three questions by the way to find out, and spots the question science cannot answer."]},"familyTip":"On a walk, take turns to ask one how or why question each. Then ask: how could we find out? Watch it, test it, or look it up?","keyIdea":"Scientists ask how, why and where questions about the world. There are different ways to find out the answers: watch something change over time, do a test, or look it up in a book or ask an expert. A question about what you like best is not one science can answer.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"How can we find out? Drag each question to watch, test or look it up, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["watch it","test it","look it up"],"items":[["What does a tadpole turn into?",0],["Which paper towel soaks up the most water?",1],["How far away is the Sun?",2],["How does the Moon change over a month?",0],["Does a toy car go faster down a steep ramp?",1],["What did dinosaurs eat?",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsciq","caption":"Flagship lab: ask questions in a garden, then find out by watching frogspawn, testing bouncing balls and looking up the Moon.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_vJO5Bxk4z-":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"The view through the lens is blurry. What should you do?","choices":["Move the lens until it looks sharp","Press the lens flat on the leaf","Stop, lenses are always blurry"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try a little closer, then a little farther.","explain":"A view is sharp only at the right distance. Move the lens a bit until the details are clear."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Can you look at the Sun through a hand lens?","choices":["No, never","Yes, for a moment","Yes, on a cloudy day"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about keeping your eyes safe.","explain":"Never look at the Sun, with or without a lens. It can hurt your eyes. A lens in sunshine can also start a fire."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Two leaves look the same from far away. How do you check?","choices":["Look closely at both, side by side","Say they are the same","Pick the bigger one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Small differences need close looking.","explain":"Look at both with a lens, side by side. Then you can say what is the same and what is different."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which is a texture word?","choices":["rough","red","round"],"answer":0,"hint":"Texture is how something feels or looks like it feels.","explain":"Rough is a texture. Red is a colour and round is a shape."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You want to see how long an ice cube takes to melt. Which tool do you use?","choices":["A timer","A ruler","A hand lens"],"answer":0,"hint":"This 'how long' is about time.","explain":"A timer measures time. A ruler measures length and a lens shows small details."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Under the lens you count {a} ants on a stone. Then {b} walk away. How many are left?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take away the ants that walked off.","explain":"{a} take away {b} leaves {=a-b} ants on the stone.","params":{"a":[5,9],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A lens makes a seed look {z} times as long. The seed is {s} mm long. How long does it look?","answer":"s*z","hint":"Add the seed's length {z} times.","explain":"{z} lots of {s} mm is {=s*z} mm. The seed itself is still {s} mm long.","params":{"s":[1,5],"z":[2,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Mia says the lens made the ant bigger. What is right?","choices":["The ant only looks bigger","The ant really grew","The ant got heavier"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take the lens away. What size is the ant?","explain":"A lens changes how the ant looks to you, not the ant itself. Without the lens it is the same small ant."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which is the best way to describe a feather?","choices":["Grey, soft and long","Pretty","A feather thing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use colour, texture and size words.","explain":"Grey is a colour, soft is a texture and long is a size. Those words tell someone what you saw."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A hand lens shows tiny hairs on a leaf. Were the hairs there before you looked?","choices":["Yes, they were too small to see","No, the lens made them","Only while the lens is there"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does a lens change the leaf, or only how it looks to your eyes?","explain":"The hairs were always there. The lens only makes them look bigger so you can see them."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which words tell a friend most about a shell?","choices":["It is brown and bumpy","It is nice","It is a good one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pick words about colour, shape or how it feels.","explain":"Brown is a colour and bumpy is a texture. Nice and good say what you think, not what you see."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"With a lens you count {a} spots on one ladybird and {b} spots on another. How many spots altogether?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Count on from the first ladybird's spots.","explain":"{a} spots and {b} spots make {=a+b} spots altogether.","params":{"a":[2,7],"b":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"You want to know how long a worm is. Which tool do you use?","choices":["A ruler","A hand lens","A timer"],"answer":0,"hint":"This 'how long' is about length, not time.","explain":"A ruler measures length. A lens shows small details and a timer measures time."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"Leaf 1 has {a} veins on one side. Leaf 2 has {b} veins on one side. How many more does leaf 1 have?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Find the difference: count on from the smaller number to the bigger one.","explain":"{a} take away {b} is {=a-b}, so leaf 1 has {=a-b} more veins.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A hand lens is a round glass you hold. It makes small things look bigger. You can see tiny bits you could not see before. The tiny bits were there all the time. The lens does not make them grow. It just helps your eyes.","If it looks blurry, move the lens a bit. Go closer or farther until it looks sharp.","Say what you see. Use words for colour, shape, size and feel. Say bumpy, round or stripy. Nice and good do not tell what you see.","Look at two things side by side to find what is the same and what is not. Use a ruler to find how long. Use a timer to see how much time. Never look at the Sun with a lens."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 7. National Curriculum in England, Key Stage 1 working scientifically: observing closely, using simple equipment, and identifying and classifying. Children use observations to describe, compare and group things.","A hand lens is a convex lens. Held close to the eye, with the object brought in until the image is sharp, it gives an enlarged view, typically 2 to 10 times. The object is unchanged: the lens reveals detail already present. If the object is too close or too far from the lens, the image blurs. Good observation uses specific vocabulary for observable properties (colour, shape, size, texture, pattern) and records similarities and differences when two objects are compared side by side. Choosing equipment means matching it to the question: a hand lens for fine detail, a ruler for length, a timer for time.","Common mistakes: thinking the lens makes the object itself bigger; pressing the lens flat on the object or giving up when the view is blurry; describing with opinion words such as nice or pretty; calling two things the same after a glance; mixing up how long (length) with how long (time); and looking at the Sun through a lens, which can damage eyes.","In the lab your child drags a lens over a leaf to find veins, edge hairs and an aphid, adjusts it until the view is sharp, chooses looking words for a shell, compares two similar leaves, sorts questions onto tools and picks the safe way to use a lens."]},"familyTip":"Take a hand lens outside and look at a leaf, some bark or a stone together. Ask for three looking words each, like bumpy, grey or stripy. Then find two leaves that look the same and look closely for one difference. Remind your child never to look at the Sun through the lens.","keyIdea":"Scientists look closely. A hand lens makes small things look bigger, so you can see details that were there all along. Move it until the view is sharp, then describe what you see with looking words: colour, shape, size, texture and pattern. Use your observations to compare and identify things, and pick the right tool for each question.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which tool answers each question? Drag each card into a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Hand lens","Ruler","Timer"],"items":[["See the tiny hairs on a leaf",0],["How long is my pencil?",1],["How long does the ice take to melt?",2],["Count the legs on an ant",0],["How tall is my plant?",1],["How long can I hop?",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaghlens","caption":"Flagship lab: drag a hand lens over a leaf to find tiny things, make it sharp, describe a shell, compare two leaves, match tools and stay safe.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
